// A program too deeply nested for any analysis to follow (cycle 0.5.7).
//
// EVERY WALK IN THIS COMPILER THAT FOLLOWS PROGRAM STRUCTURE IS BOUNDED, because
// the structure is a program's and a program can nest as deeply as somebody
// writes. What matters is what happens AT the bound, and the answer has to be the
// same for all of them: refuse.
//
// AN ANALYSIS THAT ANSWERS "NOTHING WRONG HERE" WHEN IT STOPPED LOOKING IS WORSE
// THAN NO ANALYSIS, because the property was claimed and relied on. So the escape
// checker answers "this may be a borrow", definite assignment answers "this may be
// unassigned", and both say so in their own words rather than reporting one of the
// rules — a reader who sees this has written something the checker cannot
// classify, which is a different problem from having written a defect.
//
// THIS IS THE ONLY TEST IN THE SUITE THAT IS ABOUT THE COMPILER'S LIMITS RATHER
// THAN THE LANGUAGE'S RULES. It is here because those limits are load-bearing: the
// fail-closed direction is the whole reason a bound is safe to have.

// FOUR ANALYSES REPORT, AND A FIFTH FINDING FOLLOWS FROM THE FIRST. The escape
// checker's conservative answer at the bound is "this MAY be a borrow", so the
// binding is marked as holding one and `pass x;` below is then reported as
// returning it.
//
// That second finding is the fail-closed direction working rather than a cascade
// to suppress. Silencing it would mean a program with one over-deep expression
// quietly got fewer checks than one without — and the alternative reading, "we
// could not classify this so we will assume it is fine", is the one thing a
// borrow checker may never do. The `TOO_DEEP` finding above it says why.
// expect-error: NITPICK-BORROW-007
// expect-error: NITPICK-ASSIGN-003
// expect-error: NITPICK-PICK-005
// expect-error: NITPICK-LOCK-004
// NITPICK-BORROW-001 no longer accompanies the depth refusal, and its absence
// is D-138's type narrowing working: the whole tower is `int32`, and an integer
// at ANY nesting depth still cannot be an address, so the "returned a borrow"
// cascade was a second sentence about one refusal. BORROW-007 above is the
// fail-closed half and still fires.
func:deeper_than_the_walk_will_follow = int32() {
    int32:x = 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 + 1i32 